{"issue":{"title":"Config parse errors don't name the broken file","problem":"`src/config.js` `loadConfig(path)` returns `JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'))`. When the JSON is malformed, the SyntaxError it throws says nothing about which file it came from. `src/app.js` catches the error and prints `bad config: <error.message>`, then exits 1. Under Node v22, a file whose line 3 is `  greeting: \"x\"` prints `bad config: Expected double-quoted property name in JSON at position 20 (line 3 column 3)`. That message has no file path, so a user can't tell from the output which config file is broken. The line and column come from the Node runtime, not from this repository, so other Node versions may print only a character position or nothing at all.","reason":"People with a typo in their config see the app die with a message that doesn't say which file to open or where the mistake is in it. They have to guess. The suggested fix of catching the error and returning `{}` would be worse. The app would start silently on defaults (`listening on 8080`), the typo would be hidden, and the behaviour documented in the README (`bad config: <message>` and exit 1) would break.","outcome":"A malformed config file still stops the app with exit 1 and a `bad config:` line on stderr. That line now also names the file path and the line and column of the mistake. `loadConfig` still throws on malformed JSON instead of returning a value, and valid configs load exactly as before.","expect":["Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/c.json`, where c.json contains the four lines `{`, `  \"port\": 9000,`, `  greeting: \"x\"`, `}`, exits with code 1 and prints nothing on stdout (in particular no `listening on` line).","In that same run, stderr contains `bad config:`, the full path `<tmp>/c.json` exactly as passed on the command line, the line number `3` and the column number `3` of the mistake.","Calling `loadConfig(path)` on a file containing `{port: 9000` throws instead of returning a value, and the thrown error's `message` contains `path`.","Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/ok.json`, where ok.json contains `{\"port\": 9000}`, exits 0 and prints `listening on 9000` on stdout. `loadConfig` on that file returns `{ port: 9000 }`.","`npm test` passes, and the test suite has at least one test that fails at the current base commit and checks that the error from malformed config JSON includes the file path."],"proofs":[{"line":"Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/c.json`, where c.json contains the four lines `{`, `  \"port\": 9000,`, `  greeting: \"x\"`, `}`, exits with code 1 and prints nothing on stdout (in particular no `listening on` line).","proof":"run","how":"d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\\n  \"port\": 9000,\\n  greeting: \"x\"\\n}\\n' > $d/c.json; node src/app.js $d/c.json >$d/out 2>$d/err; echo \"exit $?\"; cat $d/out  # expect exit 1 and empty stdout"},{"line":"In that same run, stderr contains `bad config:`, the full path `<tmp>/c.json` exactly as passed on the command line, the line number `3` and the column number `3` of the mistake.","proof":"run","how":"Same setup as the previous proof; then cat $d/err. Check that it contains `bad config:` and the string \"$d/c.json\", and that it gives line 3 and column 3 (e.g. `line 3 column 3` or `3:3`). Run it on the Node version the repo uses (v22 here), and also confirm the location does not depend only on Node's own message by reading src/config.js / src/app.js."},{"line":"Calling `loadConfig(path)` on a file containing `{port: 9000` throws instead of returning a value, and the thrown error's `message` contains `path`.","proof":"run","how":"d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{port: 9000' > $d/b.json; node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; try { const v = loadConfig('$d/b.json'); console.log('RETURNED', JSON.stringify(v)); process.exit(2) } catch (e) { console.log(e.message.includes('$d/b.json') ? 'OK' : 'NO PATH: ' + e.message) }\"  # expect OK"},{"line":"Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/ok.json`, where ok.json contains `{\"port\": 9000}`, exits 0 and prints `listening on 9000` on stdout. `loadConfig` on that file returns `{ port: 9000 }`.","proof":"run","how":"d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\"port\": 9000}' > $d/ok.json; node src/app.js $d/ok.json; echo \"exit $?\"; node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; console.log(JSON.stringify(loadConfig('$d/ok.json')))\"  # expect 'listening on 9000', exit 0, {\"port\":9000}"},{"line":"`npm test` passes, and the test suite has at least one test that fails at the current base commit and checks that the error from malformed config JSON includes the file path.","proof":"run","how":"Run `npm test` at the PR head and expect it to pass. Then copy the PR's test/ files onto the base commit's src/ (git checkout <base> -- src) and run `npm test`; at least one test that asserts the file path in the error must fail."}],"non_goals":["Making `loadConfig` swallow parse errors or return `{}` or the defaults for a malformed file.","Changing how a missing config file is reported (ENOENT), beyond keeping it an exit-1 `bad config:` failure.","Validating config values or schema (e.g. port type).","Supporting comments, JSON5 or other lenient config formats."],"assumptions":["The request suggests catching the parse error in loadConfig and returning {}. That is not adopted: it hides the mistake and breaks the README's documented exit 1. The request's actual goal, showing which file is broken and where, is met by reporting the path and location instead.","\"Where the mistake is\" means the line and column in the file. A PR may get these from Node's message or compute them itself, but the output must show them.","The README line `bad config: <message> and exit 1` stays valid. The README can be updated to mention the file path, but this is not required.","The path is reported as it was given (the CLI argument or the default `config.json`). It is not resolved to an absolute path."]}}